WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:05.049
This is an injection moulding machine, on the shop floor simply the press.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.203
On the left the injection unit, which melts the plastic.

3
00:00:09.951 --> 00:00:13.237
On the right the clamp unit, which holds the mould shut.

4
00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:18.545
Pellets drop from the hopper into the barrel, a steel tube with heater bands.

5
00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:24.335
Inside it turns the screw, which melts the plastic, mixes it and pushes it forward.

6
00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:29.672
The mould has two halves: one on the fixed platen, one on the moving platen.

7
00:00:30.382 --> 00:00:33.342
That platen slides along four thick tie bars.

8
00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:37.552
Inside the mould is the cavity, the shape of the part.

9
00:00:38.285 --> 00:00:40.630
The melt flows in through a small opening,

10
00:00:40.827 --> 00:00:43.532
the gate; the halves meet at the parting line.

11
00:00:44.864 --> 00:00:46.629
One cycle is called a shot.

12
00:00:47.402 --> 00:00:51.976
The mould closes, and the screw slides forward like a piston: injection.

13
00:00:53.126 --> 00:00:55.460
Then it keeps pushing: the holding pressure.

14
00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:57.965
The part shrinks as it cools,

15
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:01.494
so the screw packs in more melt until the gate freezes.

16
00:01:02.627 --> 00:01:04.566
Water in the mould cools the part.

17
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:09.588
Meanwhile the screw turns back and measures out the next shot: dosing.

18
00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:12.508
That usually costs no extra time.

19
00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:17.095
Then the mould opens, and ejector pins push the part out.

20
00:01:17.913 --> 00:01:18.633
And again.

21
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:25.030
Presses are rated by clamp force, in tonnes: the force that holds the halves shut.

22
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.369
The melt pushes them apart at hundreds of bar,

23
00:01:28.566 --> 00:01:31.678
and one bar is about a kilo per square centimetre.

24
00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:36.665
The force needed depends on the projected area: the part’s shadow,

25
00:01:36.804 --> 00:01:38.081
seen from the press.

26
00:01:38.811 --> 00:01:43.943
This housing is 15 by 20 centimetres: 300 square centimetres.

27
00:01:44.997 --> 00:01:49.734
Rule of thumb: 0.3 to 0.6 tonnes per square centimetre,

28
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.819
depending on the plastic; here 0.4, so 120 tonnes.

29
00:01:55.532 --> 00:02:03.067
With 2 cavities 240, plus 10 to 20 percent margin: a 280 or 300 tonne press.

30
00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:04.983
Too little force,

31
00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:08.420
and the mould opens a hair’s breadth at the parting line:

32
00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:10.336
plastic leaks out as flash.

33
00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:13.706
So not on a free 200 tonne press.

34
00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:19.793
Now the screw: about 20 times as long as it is thick, with three zones.

35
00:02:20.526 --> 00:02:24.799
At the back, the feed zone: deep flights draw the cold pellets in.

36
00:02:25.829 --> 00:02:28.592
Then the compression zone: the channel gets shallower,

37
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.122
so the plastic is squeezed and kneaded until it melts.

38
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:37.581
At the front, the metering zone makes the melt evenly hot and well mixed.

39
00:02:38.294 --> 00:02:41.092
The check ring on the tip closes during injection.

40
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.107
Most of the heat comes from friction,

41
00:02:44.328 --> 00:02:48.229
not from the heater bands: they mainly keep the barrel at temperature.

42
00:02:49.556 --> 00:02:53.248
While dosing, the melt in front of the tip pushes the screw back,

43
00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:56.592
up to the set dosing stroke: exactly one shot.

44
00:02:57.358 --> 00:03:00.202
If dosing outlasts cooling, the press waits.

45
00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:04.292
The residence time, how long the plastic stays hot,

46
00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:07.683
is roughly the shots in the barrel times the cycle time.

47
00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:14.095
The barrel holds 600 grams, a shot is 150 grams: 4 shots.

48
00:03:14.813 --> 00:03:18.435
At 30 seconds, that is 120 seconds.

49
00:03:19.544 --> 00:03:23.422
A 60 gram part: 10 shots, so 300 seconds.

50
00:03:24.122 --> 00:03:29.369
If the data sheet says 4 minutes at most, that is too long, and black specks appear.

51
00:03:30.097 --> 00:03:32.477
That part belongs on a smaller press.

52
00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.752
Choose the press with four checks.

53
00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:41.458
One, clamp force: area times cavity pressure, the average pressure in the cavity,

54
00:03:41.656 --> 00:03:44.094
usually 300 to 600 bar.

55
00:03:44.817 --> 00:03:53.141
A lid of 380 square centimetres at 0.45 tonnes per square centimetre: 171 tonnes,

56
00:03:53.315 --> 00:03:55.649
197 with margin.

57
00:03:56.623 --> 00:04:01.941
Two, the shot volume: all the parts plus the runner, here 100 cubic centimetres.

58
00:04:02.926 --> 00:04:07.268
Divide by the barrel’s maximum shot volume: the barrel utilisation.

59
00:04:08.051 --> 00:04:11.684
Aim for 20 to 80 percent, ideally 40 to 60.

60
00:04:12.387 --> 00:04:19.063
In a barrel of 120 that is 83 percent: too much, the screw cannot melt it in time.

61
00:04:19.749 --> 00:04:22.605
In one of 250: 40 percent.

62
00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:24.915
Three, the mould height:

63
00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.711
the closed mould’s thickness must lie within the press’s range.

64
00:04:29.408 --> 00:04:32.009
And the mould must fit between the tie bars.

65
00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:36.089
Four, the opening stroke: how far the press opens,

66
00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:39.085
often twice the part depth plus room for a gripper.

67
00:04:39.817 --> 00:04:43.660
And bigger is not safer: too much force squeezes the vents shut.

68
00:04:45.086 --> 00:04:48.848
This press is hydraulic: a pump drives oil to the cylinders.

69
00:04:49.565 --> 00:04:52.050
Robust, and large forces come cheap.

70
00:04:52.774 --> 00:04:55.270
Electric presses usually close with a toggle.

71
00:04:56.273 --> 00:04:59.826
An all-electric press has a servo motor for each axis.

72
00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.397
It only uses power when it moves, repeats more precisely and leaks no oil.

73
00:05:06.425 --> 00:05:11.627
A hybrid machine combines both, usually an electric screw with a hydraulic clamp.

74
00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:17.139
Compare energy as specific energy consumption: kilowatt-hours per kilo.

75
00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:19.579
Studies differ, so measure it.

76
00:05:20.301 --> 00:05:27.429
Say 0.6 against 0.3: on a hundred and eighty thousand kilos a year, at twenty cents,

77
00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.390
that saves ten thousand eight hundred euros.

78
00:05:31.126 --> 00:05:35.340
Forty thousand euros extra pays back in 3.7 years.

79
00:05:36.681 --> 00:05:39.502
In two-component moulding two injection units

80
00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:42.637
mould a hard and a soft plastic into one part,

81
00:05:42.811 --> 00:05:44.425
often with a rotating mould.

82
00:05:45.221 --> 00:05:47.113
That saves an assembly step.

83
00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:52.841
In insert moulding the plastic is moulded around a metal part placed in the mould,

84
00:05:52.957 --> 00:05:54.339
such as a threaded bush.

85
00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.999
Both need more complex moulds.
